WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:02.703
(beaming music)

00:00:09.009 --> 00:00:11.445
- Okay, so I want 
to introduce to you

00:00:11.445 --> 00:00:14.882
a guy that you guys 
know very, very well,

00:00:16.049 --> 00:00:17.384
Mr. Aaron Price.

00:00:19.353 --> 00:00:21.221
We're making a 
very special introduction

00:00:21.221 --> 00:00:23.690
for my main 
man GaryVee, alright?

00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:26.593
(cheering)

00:00:26.593 --> 00:00:28.395
- Hello, there we go.

00:00:28.395 --> 00:00:29.997
What's up, everybody?

00:00:29.997 --> 00:00:32.366
(cheering)

00:00:32.366 --> 00:00:34.201
- I got the captain's chair.
- [Gary] Hello.

00:00:34.201 --> 00:00:35.302
- What up, Jersey?

00:00:35.302 --> 00:00:39.673
- How many people here
know the Gary Vaynerchuk?

00:00:40.574 --> 00:00:42.843
(audience cheering)

00:00:42.843 --> 00:00:45.846
How many people have been
provided what he loves to call,

00:00:45.846 --> 00:00:47.681
I think it's so 
appropriately identified,

00:00:47.681 --> 00:00:49.583
value from his content?

00:00:49.583 --> 00:00:53.287
(audience cheering)

00:00:53.287 --> 00:00:54.288
- Thank you.

00:00:54.288 --> 00:00:55.689
- If you don't know Gary--

00:00:55.689 --> 00:00:59.026
- Before we do that, let's clap
it up for this fucking day.

00:00:59.026 --> 00:01:01.428
How nice is this?

00:01:01.428 --> 00:01:03.530
That's the real 
star of the show.

00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:04.864
Forget the panels.

00:01:04.864 --> 00:01:07.801
The fucking sun came out.

00:01:07.801 --> 00:01:09.670
- And that brings us
to our first question.

00:01:09.670 --> 00:01:10.337
- Yes.

00:01:10.337 --> 00:01:12.105
- Gary talks about 
knowing his audience.

00:01:12.105 --> 00:01:12.940
- Yes.

00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:15.742
- There's a varied
group of attendees here.

00:01:15.742 --> 00:01:17.544
- Yes.
- Corporates, maybe some kids.

00:01:17.544 --> 00:01:18.378
- Yep.

00:01:18.378 --> 00:01:19.680
- And we're cursing.

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:20.914
- Yeah, I always curse.
- Yeah, I know.

00:01:20.914 --> 00:01:22.416
- I like cursing.
- We've talked about this.

00:01:22.416 --> 00:01:22.950
- Yes.

00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:25.385
- If you could prevent it, 
would you?

00:01:25.385 --> 00:01:27.220
- I mean, look, 
I've been on TV 200 times.

00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:27.955
I haven't cursed.

00:01:27.955 --> 00:01:31.191
I understand it's inappropriate,
and I'm not unaware

00:01:31.191 --> 00:01:34.328
that 20 people just now are
like, "Oh, that's off-color.

00:01:34.328 --> 00:01:35.395
"I don't like that."

00:01:35.395 --> 00:01:39.933
I respect that, I have
empathy for how I'm consumed.

00:01:39.933 --> 00:01:43.704
It's just when I see this many
people, I default into that,

00:01:43.704 --> 00:01:46.907
I default into my Jersey
stuff, I default into

00:01:46.907 --> 00:01:48.642
what Eddie Murphy 
and Richard Pryor did

00:01:48.642 --> 00:01:50.143
that I was obsessed 
with as a kid,

00:01:50.143 --> 00:01:51.411
so that's just what happens.

00:01:51.411 --> 00:01:54.448
And at some level, I think
a lot of you know this,

00:01:54.448 --> 00:01:56.817
at some level in life, whether
you got lucky like I did

00:01:56.817 --> 00:01:59.619
at a very young age, 
or when you're 90,

00:01:59.619 --> 00:02:02.489
somewhere along the line
you start realizing that

00:02:02.489 --> 00:02:05.359
it's not about who cares
what other people think?

00:02:05.359 --> 00:02:08.127
It's if you feel good 
about your intent

00:02:08.127 --> 00:02:10.396
and don't think you're
trying to do something wrong,

00:02:10.396 --> 00:02:13.066
you have to let the chips
fall where they fall.

00:02:13.066 --> 00:02:14.434
And so for me, 
that's what it is.

00:02:14.434 --> 00:02:18.839
It's, I'm not upset or
disappointed or confused why

00:02:18.839 --> 00:02:22.042
somebody doesn't like the
bravado or the cursing,

00:02:22.042 --> 00:02:24.444
I just have made a choice,

00:02:24.444 --> 00:02:26.747
that I'm not trying 
to do anything wrong.

00:02:26.747 --> 00:02:30.150
It's how I communicate and
you just have to move forward.

00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:31.318
- [Aaron] And I'm not 
criticizing you.

00:02:31.318 --> 00:02:34.054
I'm thinking about, there
are strategic relationships

00:02:34.054 --> 00:02:35.255
of potential--

00:02:35.255 --> 00:02:36.556
- I know how to make money.

00:02:36.556 --> 00:02:38.658
I'm never scared 
of losing money.

00:02:38.658 --> 00:02:40.560
- Have you ever lost a deal
because of the language?

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:41.628
- [Gary] 100%.

00:02:41.628 --> 00:02:42.662
- Yes.
- [Gary] Yes.

00:02:42.662 --> 00:02:44.464
- Didn't care?
- Nope.

00:02:44.464 --> 00:02:45.465
- Where you from originally?

00:02:45.465 --> 00:02:47.801
- Because it's a 
net-net score, right?

00:02:47.801 --> 00:02:49.736
This is a good thing for
a lot of the entrepreneurs

00:02:49.736 --> 00:02:51.004
and business people out here.

00:02:51.004 --> 00:02:54.408
Way too many people play in
what's only in front of them

00:02:54.408 --> 00:02:56.877
without understanding 
the net-net score.

00:02:56.877 --> 00:02:59.413
So, seven years ago, 
I started an agency

00:02:59.413 --> 00:03:02.416
that was selling people
something they didn't want.

00:03:02.416 --> 00:03:04.751
It wouldn't have been hard for
me to figure out how to sell

00:03:04.751 --> 00:03:09.156
PR, or television, or
SEO, but I didn't want to

00:03:09.156 --> 00:03:10.624
because I knew where 
the puck was going.

00:03:10.624 --> 00:03:12.526
I knew where social was going.

00:03:12.526 --> 00:03:17.197
And so I was okay with losing
deal after deal after deal

00:03:17.197 --> 00:03:20.367
in 2009, '10, '11, which
I did, because I knew

00:03:20.367 --> 00:03:23.170
I was affecting the people
in the room with my thesis

00:03:23.170 --> 00:03:24.905
and I knew that 
I was gonna be right.

00:03:24.905 --> 00:03:28.842
And now in 2016, '17, '15,
a lot of those same people

00:03:28.842 --> 00:03:31.278
who laughed me out of the
room, who thought I was wrong,

00:03:31.278 --> 00:03:33.213
are coming back and
giving me a lot of money

00:03:33.213 --> 00:03:34.915
because I played 
out to be right.

00:03:34.915 --> 00:03:37.818
So in the net-net of 
a decade, I won,

00:03:37.818 --> 00:03:40.754
even though maybe I lost in
that year or two or three.

00:03:40.754 --> 00:03:42.789
- So, you have the good fortune

00:03:42.789 --> 00:03:44.758
of being more right than wrong.

00:03:44.758 --> 00:03:45.926
- [Gary] Yes.

00:03:45.926 --> 00:03:47.928
- Most people are
more wrong than right.

00:03:47.928 --> 00:03:49.396
- [Gary] That's why 
I'm sitting up here.

00:03:49.396 --> 00:03:50.564
(laughs)
- How--

00:03:50.564 --> 00:03:51.598
And that's just true.

00:03:51.598 --> 00:03:52.432
- Yeah, that's the game.

00:03:52.432 --> 00:03:53.934
- That's the game, that's right.

00:03:53.934 --> 00:03:55.836
By the way, and you 
know what I love?

00:03:55.836 --> 00:03:59.473
That if I do my videos 
and spew my shit

00:03:59.473 --> 00:04:00.674
and for the next two years

00:04:00.674 --> 00:04:04.111
I'm just wrong, wrong,
wrong, wrong, wrong.

00:04:04.111 --> 00:04:06.179
Then I don't deserve 
to sit up here,

00:04:06.179 --> 00:04:08.882
and so I love the 
meritocracy of that.

00:04:08.882 --> 00:04:12.419
I'm not sitting up here
because I'm cute or I'm cool.

00:04:12.419 --> 00:04:14.821
I'm sitting up here because
I've been right enough

00:04:14.821 --> 00:04:17.591
and I bring people value in
that rightness, if they decide.

00:04:17.591 --> 00:04:20.994
I've had 40 people in
the last month email me

00:04:20.994 --> 00:04:23.530
that they've made 10s
of thousands of dollars

00:04:23.530 --> 00:04:26.766
being a Snapchat filter designer
when they had eight bucks

00:04:26.766 --> 00:04:28.101
in their bank account.

00:04:28.101 --> 00:04:30.770
That's why they wanna drive
from Philly and see me.

00:04:30.770 --> 00:04:31.671
Right?

00:04:31.671 --> 00:04:35.609
That's why, 
not because I'm clever.

00:04:35.609 --> 00:04:39.513
Because I realize that if I can
bring more value than I take

00:04:39.513 --> 00:04:42.015
back in return, 
then I'm valuable.

00:04:42.015 --> 00:04:45.085
And I think that people are
way too short-sighted and

00:04:45.085 --> 00:04:46.453
are looking for 
short-term results

00:04:46.453 --> 00:04:48.088
and are not looking 
to provide that,

00:04:48.088 --> 00:04:51.057
and are looking at from
what's in it for me?

00:04:51.057 --> 00:04:52.726
and that's just not how I see it

00:04:52.726 --> 00:04:55.228
because I don't think
business is a one-year game.

00:04:55.228 --> 00:04:57.797
If I did, by the way,
let me save you time.

00:04:57.797 --> 00:05:00.967
If you start seeing 
my behavior cashing in

00:05:00.967 --> 00:05:03.970
and trying to extract, then
you know that I've decided

00:05:03.970 --> 00:05:07.807
in 24, 36 months, it's a wrap.

00:05:07.807 --> 00:05:08.441
Right?

00:05:08.441 --> 00:05:11.011
That I don't need it anymore,
but doing the right thing

00:05:11.011 --> 00:05:13.079
upfront always brings 
long-term value.

00:05:13.079 --> 00:05:15.048
- So tell people when, you
had a book launch, right?

00:05:15.048 --> 00:05:15.882
- Yep.

00:05:15.882 --> 00:05:17.250
- There was a lot of lead-up--

00:05:17.250 --> 00:05:20.086
- Yes, I was selling 
hard for 3, 4 months.

00:05:20.086 --> 00:05:22.255
- Tell people, if they
haven't read the book,

00:05:22.255 --> 00:05:25.292
in a minute or less,
what's the jab and when is

00:05:25.292 --> 00:05:27.160
the appropriate time to
bring the right hook?

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:30.063
- Oh, so you mean 
Jab, Jab, Jab, Right Hook?

00:05:30.063 --> 00:05:31.431
You know, 
it's a thesis I believe in.

00:05:31.431 --> 00:05:34.000
Give value, give value,
give value, and then ask.

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:37.137
I think a lot of you out
there that do know my stuff,

00:05:37.137 --> 00:05:40.073
some of you have confused
it as give, give, give

00:05:40.073 --> 00:05:41.041
and then take.

00:05:41.041 --> 00:05:43.843
I've had a lot of people ask
me why they didn't convert

00:05:43.843 --> 00:05:46.279
and when I look at their
tactics, it's 'cause they're

00:05:46.279 --> 00:05:48.348
trying to take, 
they're not asking.

00:05:48.348 --> 00:05:51.818
So for me, if you've watched
every video that was free,

00:05:51.818 --> 00:05:53.920
if I emailed you 
back four times,

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:56.089
if I've responded to
you six times on social,

00:05:56.089 --> 00:05:58.124
if I shook your 
hand four times here,

00:05:58.124 --> 00:06:00.260
and then book number five
comes out and you decide

00:06:00.260 --> 00:06:03.430
not to spend that $18,
I'm not crippled by that.

00:06:03.430 --> 00:06:05.999
Because I don't think any
of my actions that I'm doing

00:06:05.999 --> 00:06:08.235
entitle me to anything from you.

00:06:08.235 --> 00:06:11.838
I think what they entitle
me to is a solid ask

00:06:11.838 --> 00:06:14.507
that feels appropriate, but
if I convert, that's really

00:06:14.507 --> 00:06:16.443
up to the quality of what
I'm delivering for you

00:06:16.443 --> 00:06:17.911
at that moment, and I think that

00:06:17.911 --> 00:06:19.980
way too many 
people are entitled.

00:06:19.980 --> 00:06:21.481
I think way too 
many people are entitled,

00:06:21.481 --> 00:06:24.484
I think way too many people
complain, it just is what it is.

00:06:24.484 --> 00:06:27.187
The market doesn't care, right?

00:06:27.187 --> 00:06:30.757
The market, the game, doesn't
care where you grew up,

00:06:30.757 --> 00:06:34.427
or your mom didn't do this
for you, or you don't have

00:06:34.427 --> 00:06:36.429
any good developers around you.

00:06:36.429 --> 00:06:37.664
It just doesn't care.

00:06:37.664 --> 00:06:39.899
It's the market, and you
have to adjust to that

00:06:39.899 --> 00:06:41.601
and so I equally do that.

00:06:41.601 --> 00:06:44.437
When you're at the top or
doing well, you're only as good

00:06:44.437 --> 00:06:45.605
as your last at-bat.

00:06:45.605 --> 00:06:47.340
It doesn't matter 
that I've been good

00:06:47.340 --> 00:06:49.042
at business for 20 years.

00:06:49.042 --> 00:06:53.246
If I suck in 2017 and '18,
I'm gonna lose.

00:06:53.246 --> 00:06:56.049
- So, there's a, you know
the whole ethos of Propeller.

00:06:56.049 --> 00:06:57.450
- Yes.
- Is what we say idle

00:06:57.450 --> 00:06:59.252
ideas don't fly, right?

00:06:59.252 --> 00:07:00.887
And there's a lot of people, 
who I wonder,

00:07:00.887 --> 00:07:02.722
are sittin' in maybe a cubicle

00:07:02.722 --> 00:07:04.624
and watchin' your stuff,
gettin' all fired up,

00:07:04.624 --> 00:07:07.127
but not taking their
ideas to the next level.

00:07:07.127 --> 00:07:07.994
What do you tell those guys?

00:07:07.994 --> 00:07:09.229
Some of those guys, 
by the way, feel like

00:07:09.229 --> 00:07:10.330
maybe they can't take the risk,

00:07:10.330 --> 00:07:11.164
maybe they've got 
young kids at home,

00:07:11.164 --> 00:07:13.566
they don't have the
financial where-with-all.

00:07:13.566 --> 00:07:15.969
- I tell them that I respect
them and I understand.

00:07:15.969 --> 00:07:18.338
And I tell them to do
the best job they can

00:07:18.338 --> 00:07:20.473
to try to feel what 
it might feel like in

00:07:20.473 --> 00:07:23.076
30 or 40 years if they 
don't at least try.

00:07:23.076 --> 00:07:26.112
I think regret is 
the scariest feeling.

00:07:26.112 --> 00:07:27.247
And I think,

00:07:27.247 --> 00:07:30.650
I think if you really
analyze 70, 80, 90-year-olds

00:07:30.650 --> 00:07:33.253
that lost a lot of money,
don't have a lot of money

00:07:33.253 --> 00:07:34.954
versus ones that are okay,

00:07:34.954 --> 00:07:37.090
but have a lot of regret
of never going for it.

00:07:37.090 --> 00:07:39.125
It's a pretty interesting
conversation.

00:07:39.125 --> 00:07:40.760
Even the cameraman here
is givin' me a little love

00:07:40.760 --> 00:07:41.594
on that shake.

00:07:41.594 --> 00:07:42.829
It's an interesting
conversation,

00:07:42.829 --> 00:07:44.431
that when I look at 
an 80-year-old

00:07:44.431 --> 00:07:47.334
who was middle-class
but loaded with regret

00:07:47.334 --> 00:07:49.536
because he didn't have
the guts to go for it,

00:07:49.536 --> 00:07:51.471
versus somebody who
didn't have a lot of money

00:07:51.471 --> 00:07:53.039
and had to struggle and
things of that nature

00:07:53.039 --> 00:07:54.774
like it's just funny,
the person with regret

00:07:54.774 --> 00:07:58.111
feels more in pain and
I try to talk about that

00:07:58.111 --> 00:08:00.747
because, ya know, 
I just think it's real.

00:08:00.747 --> 00:08:01.881
And then, you know,

00:08:01.881 --> 00:08:05.118
I had video recently go
quite crazy on Facebook,

00:08:05.118 --> 00:08:07.987
that I was talkin' to 
22 to 25 year olds.

00:08:07.987 --> 00:08:10.623
I think it's extremely practical

00:08:10.623 --> 00:08:13.927
to be high risk in the first
five years out of college

00:08:13.927 --> 00:08:15.895
even with debt sitting there.

00:08:15.895 --> 00:08:18.732
Because at 27, 28 you're
more than welcome to get that

00:08:18.732 --> 00:08:21.601
standard stable job and
just chip away with it.

00:08:21.601 --> 00:08:23.403
But at that first five
years, you can go after

00:08:23.403 --> 00:08:25.605
all your dreams, and ambitions
and thoughts and needs.

00:08:25.605 --> 00:08:29.242
And so, it's an interesting
theme what people react to.

00:08:29.976 --> 00:08:31.845
I think a lot of it has
to do with regret.

00:08:31.845 --> 00:08:34.114
And I think my

00:08:34.114 --> 00:08:37.049
intuition about 
being an old soul,

00:08:37.049 --> 00:08:40.253
and spending time with
older demo people

00:08:40.253 --> 00:08:43.490
has given me some vibe 
that makes me say

00:08:43.490 --> 00:08:45.825
"Shit, it doesn't 
matter what you think

00:08:45.825 --> 00:08:46.893
"when you're 20, 30, 40."

00:08:46.893 --> 00:08:49.028
It matters, but you're
makin' decisions on how you

00:08:49.028 --> 00:08:50.830
think you're gonna 
feel at 60 and 70.

00:08:50.830 --> 00:08:52.098
And if you actually listened

00:08:52.098 --> 00:08:53.800
to 60, 70, and 
80-year-old people,

00:08:53.800 --> 00:08:55.435
it's just not how it plays out.

00:08:55.435 --> 00:08:58.805
So, why don't you figure
that out and reverse it back.

00:08:58.805 --> 00:09:00.140
- What do you think about the,

00:09:00.140 --> 00:09:02.208
you know, start-ups
are hot shit right now.

00:09:02.208 --> 00:09:03.309
You got a lot of young kids

00:09:03.309 --> 00:09:05.311
and older people 
raising capital.

00:09:05.311 --> 00:09:06.379
But what do you think,

00:09:06.379 --> 00:09:07.947
you know, 
do you think it's over--

00:09:07.947 --> 00:09:09.582
- [Gary] I think it's--
- celebrated?

00:09:09.582 --> 00:09:11.618
- It's definitely 
over celebrated.

00:09:11.618 --> 00:09:12.786
- [Aaron] Welcome to Propeller.

00:09:12.786 --> 00:09:14.154
- Like,
(crowd laughing)

00:09:14.154 --> 00:09:15.855
Like, it's the most
over-celebrated thing

00:09:15.855 --> 00:09:16.956
going on right now.

00:09:16.956 --> 00:09:17.957
Like, yo, I'm a founder.

00:09:17.957 --> 00:09:18.825
Cool.

00:09:18.825 --> 00:09:20.160
Like, you've raised 
a lot of money,

00:09:20.160 --> 00:09:21.394
you've never 
accomplished anything.

00:09:21.394 --> 00:09:24.597
Like "I'm an entrepreneur"
is like sayin',

00:09:24.597 --> 00:09:26.933
"I sing songs" right?

00:09:26.933 --> 00:09:29.469
Like, but do you make 
money singing songs?

00:09:29.469 --> 00:09:31.104
Are you Beyonce?

00:09:31.104 --> 00:09:33.273
Like, just because 
you're an entrepreneur,

00:09:33.273 --> 00:09:34.140
doesn't mean anything.

00:09:34.140 --> 00:09:35.642
And everyone's like, 
"Yo, I'm an entrepreneur."

00:09:35.642 --> 00:09:39.212
I'm like "Cool, are you
a winning entrepreneur?

00:09:39.212 --> 00:09:41.614
"Are you a money-making
entrepreneur?"

00:09:41.614 --> 00:09:44.150
Like "Will you be an
entrepreneur in four years

00:09:44.150 --> 00:09:45.985
"when the economic 
meltdown comes

00:09:45.985 --> 00:09:48.521
or are you gonna be
workin' at J.P. Morgan?"

00:09:48.521 --> 00:09:51.558
So, I think shit's ugly.

00:09:51.558 --> 00:09:53.626
I think a lot of 
people I just met

00:09:53.626 --> 00:09:55.495
will be out of business 'cause
they don't have a business,

00:09:55.495 --> 00:09:57.630
they have an idea and 
they're tryin' to raise money.

00:09:57.630 --> 00:09:59.132
