00:18:40.379 --> 00:18:42.307
I would suffocate if 
I couldn't do this,

00:18:42.307 --> 00:18:45.257
I love when bad days,
yesterday was a shit day,

00:18:45.257 --> 00:18:47.280
the shit, fuckin' shit,

00:18:47.280 --> 00:18:49.082
like 21 hours--
- [Aaron] Hey, how was yesterday?

00:18:49.082 --> 00:18:50.349
- Shit.
(audience laughing)

00:18:50.349 --> 00:18:51.658
Like, so many bad things
happened yesterday,

00:18:51.658 --> 00:18:55.461
yesterday was shit,
like on--like terrible.

00:18:55.461 --> 00:18:56.930
But I love it because--
- [Aaron] Business or personal?

00:18:56.930 --> 00:19:01.073
- Business, but like, it's
great, it's much better

00:19:01.073 --> 00:19:04.577
than fuckin' doin' something
that I don't wanna do.

00:19:04.577 --> 00:19:08.514
- [Aaron] But there must be 
times, when maybe you don't feel

00:19:08.514 --> 00:19:10.249
depressed, but you feel
the struggle, right,

00:19:10.249 --> 00:19:11.918
you feel like yesterday.
- The struggle?

00:19:11.918 --> 00:19:14.320
Every second. Every day.
- Right, so you wake up today,

00:19:14.320 --> 00:19:15.822
right, what keeps you goin', 
what keeps you

00:19:15.822 --> 00:19:18.541
jumpin' outta bed?
- I have no other option.

00:19:18.541 --> 00:19:22.345
This is my oxygen, I wasn't
into other shit like you were,

00:19:22.345 --> 00:19:24.747
this is all I ever knew, I'd
fuckin' pick flowers and sell

00:19:24.747 --> 00:19:27.650
it at four, lemonade--my hobby
in the summer was washing

00:19:27.650 --> 00:19:29.686
people's cars for money.

00:19:29.686 --> 00:19:31.554
- How many people here did
stuff like that, wash cars,

00:19:31.554 --> 00:19:34.223
lemonade stands, 
hack shit at school?

00:19:34.223 --> 00:19:36.693
- And so you know, that's
how they're wired, like me,

00:19:36.693 --> 00:19:38.861
like that's all you knew,
like my brain never thought,

00:19:38.861 --> 00:19:40.730
oh, let's go play Nintendo,
my brain was like,

00:19:40.730 --> 00:19:43.499
yo, let's go buy a Nintendo
and sell it, I'm gonna rent

00:19:43.499 --> 00:19:46.436
cartridges, like that's
just all I've ever knew,

00:19:46.436 --> 00:19:48.965
I don't have another gear,
I'm fuckin' basic,

00:19:48.965 --> 00:19:51.874
I'm one-dimensional.
- Man, I think that's awesome,

00:19:51.874 --> 00:19:53.509
I think that's why you're
so celebrated, right,

00:19:53.509 --> 00:19:56.079
but there's a lot of people
here who aren't wired that way,

00:19:56.079 --> 00:19:57.480
and I don't think we wanna--

00:19:57.480 --> 00:19:59.115
- And they're lucky, this sucks.

00:19:59.115 --> 00:20:00.216
(audience laughing)
- But we don't wanna

00:20:00.216 --> 00:20:01.284
discourage them, 
there's some successes--

00:20:01.284 --> 00:20:03.889
- I'm not discouraging them,
I just said they're lucky,

00:20:03.889 --> 00:20:07.713
do you know how much 
I wish I wasn't so insane?

00:20:07.713 --> 00:20:09.482
Like, do you know how
much more fun it would be,

00:20:09.482 --> 00:20:13.252
I would be thrilled to leave
20% of the money and the action

00:20:13.252 --> 00:20:16.289
and the selfies on the table,
for a couple more vacations,

00:20:16.289 --> 00:20:19.459
to watch, it'd be fun to know
what the fuck's on Netflix,

00:20:19.459 --> 00:20:22.962
you know, like, I wish, 
by the way, I'm not joking,

00:20:22.962 --> 00:20:26.165
I'm not telling people to be
like me, this isn't great,

00:20:26.165 --> 00:20:28.261
this is just who I am.

00:20:28.261 --> 00:20:29.729
- Let's talk about that
self-awareness, right,

00:20:29.729 --> 00:20:30.930
'cause that's the key to this.

00:20:30.930 --> 00:20:33.932
You are authentically you.
- Yes.

00:20:33.932 --> 00:20:35.367
I try very hard.
- And I think the key point

00:20:35.367 --> 00:20:37.130
here is we're not saying, 
be like Gary.

00:20:37.130 --> 00:20:38.610
- [Gary] No, I'm saying, 
be like you.

00:20:38.610 --> 00:20:39.669
- Yeah, and how do people find

00:20:39.669 --> 00:20:41.207
that, how do people 
find that thing that's

00:20:41.207 --> 00:20:43.176
authentically them?
- I don't know.

00:20:43.176 --> 00:20:44.443
I really don't.
- How do they know when

00:20:44.443 --> 00:20:46.379
it's not?
- The results.

00:20:48.110 --> 00:20:49.646
- [Aaron] There are some people 
who will be authentically

00:20:49.646 --> 00:20:52.061
themselves in a shitty business,
and it won't work--

00:20:52.061 --> 00:20:55.238
- Great, they may have
everything else going for them.

00:20:55.238 --> 00:20:56.472
- And you tell those guys what?

00:20:56.472 --> 00:20:57.907
Try again?
- Mazel Tov.

00:20:57.907 --> 00:21:00.009
No, I mean, what do you mean,
like, everything's going

00:21:00.009 --> 00:21:02.111
great for you, but you
can't build a business?

00:21:02.111 --> 00:21:03.913
Great, get an awesome 
job that you like,

00:21:03.913 --> 00:21:06.482
and your life is fuckin' set.

00:21:06.482 --> 00:21:08.551
- How many times 
should people, you know,

00:21:08.551 --> 00:21:11.554
this here today is a result
of 15 years of hustle,

00:21:11.554 --> 00:21:14.690
with one good business
and a dozen failures.

00:21:14.690 --> 00:21:17.326
I kept at it, and I think we
got something pretty special

00:21:17.326 --> 00:21:18.394
here.
- Yep.

00:21:18.394 --> 00:21:20.129
- A lot of people 
don't keep at it.

00:21:20.129 --> 00:21:21.564
- Right.
- How, when do you know

00:21:21.564 --> 00:21:23.399
when to throw in the towel?

00:21:23.399 --> 00:21:24.901
- You know, I think that's
a personal question for

00:21:24.901 --> 00:21:27.403
everybody, right, There's
family dynamics, you may have

00:21:27.403 --> 00:21:31.908
a parent pass away, it might
be a good thing, you hit a

00:21:31.908 --> 00:21:34.343
zone where you're content,
and this is what you want,

00:21:34.343 --> 00:21:38.414
I don't think, to me that, you
can see my tone has changed,

00:21:38.414 --> 00:21:40.850
I think is a very personal
question. You know,

00:21:40.850 --> 00:21:43.152
it's hard to judge. I'm sure
there's plenty of people

00:21:43.152 --> 00:21:45.488
who gave up one month before,
after doing it for three

00:21:45.488 --> 00:21:47.924
years, and that next month
was gonna be the moment,

00:21:47.924 --> 00:21:50.459
and that's sad, but I think
we all know there's so many

00:21:50.459 --> 00:21:52.962
people that have gone
too far and too long,

00:21:52.962 --> 00:21:55.198
and that hurt them.
I think that's comes down

00:21:55.198 --> 00:21:57.333
to self-awareness 
and looking at results.

00:21:57.333 --> 00:22:00.002
Here's what I would say, guys,

00:22:00.002 --> 00:22:02.805
it doesn't matter what I just
said for the last 20 minutes,

00:22:02.805 --> 00:22:04.941
it doesn't matter the reverse
of my argument for the last

00:22:04.941 --> 00:22:07.977
20 minutes, it's a constant
storm to try to figure out

00:22:07.977 --> 00:22:10.112
what you're about, 
and you change.

00:22:10.112 --> 00:22:11.914
At 26, you're all about the
hustle, you're gonna be a

00:22:11.914 --> 00:22:14.050
billionaire, this and that,
and then you go to the bar one

00:22:14.050 --> 00:22:16.886
night, and you fall in love,
and it changes what you care

00:22:16.886 --> 00:22:18.888
about, right, and then you
have a child, and then this

00:22:18.888 --> 00:22:21.657
happens, and then that
happens, things change.

00:22:21.657 --> 00:22:24.360
You just have to always
consistently try to figure out

00:22:24.360 --> 00:22:26.896
what's driving you, and
not because other people

00:22:26.896 --> 00:22:29.031
are watching, and not
because that's what your dad

00:22:29.031 --> 00:22:31.868
wants, and not because that's
what you said was gonna

00:22:31.868 --> 00:22:33.936
happen, and it doesn't look
like it and your family's

00:22:33.936 --> 00:22:36.239
gonna judge you, you just
have to be as real with

00:22:36.239 --> 00:22:38.875
yourself as possible, and
that is a very difficult

00:22:38.875 --> 00:22:42.278
struggle, but when you're
not, you create enormous

00:22:42.278 --> 00:22:44.747
vulnerability and unhappiness.

00:22:46.305 --> 00:22:49.642
- [Aaron] And that can be 
very problematic, personally

00:22:49.642 --> 00:22:52.765
on the home life, right, 
I mean how do you balance that?

00:22:52.765 --> 00:22:54.934
- Over-communication.

00:22:54.934 --> 00:22:57.703
You know, Lizzie and I talk,
and I never give people

00:22:57.703 --> 00:23:00.173
relationship advice, and
people ask me all the time,

00:23:00.173 --> 00:23:02.708
'cause a lot of you know
that I work so much,

00:23:02.708 --> 00:23:05.378
and everybody has 
different relationships,

00:23:05.378 --> 00:23:07.413
and I have to over-communicate
with my wife and

00:23:07.413 --> 00:23:10.082
understand how she's ticking
and what's making her happy

00:23:10.082 --> 00:23:13.486
and what's not, and what she
said last week is irrelevant

00:23:13.486 --> 00:23:15.321
to tomorrow, you know?
People are like,

00:23:15.321 --> 00:23:16.656
oh, you said you were
gonna support me,

00:23:16.656 --> 00:23:19.058
that was two years ago, 
and things change.

00:23:19.058 --> 00:23:21.394
And so it's a constant
stream of communication,

00:23:21.394 --> 00:23:22.895
no different than what you
should be having with your

00:23:22.895 --> 00:23:25.031
employees and your partners,
and you do that with your

00:23:25.031 --> 00:23:27.834
spouse and your partner,
and things of that nature.

00:23:27.834 --> 00:23:29.068
- What's the average
time you get home at the

00:23:29.068 --> 00:23:31.671
end of each day?
- 11 PM.

00:23:31.671 --> 00:23:33.406
- And you're offline weekends.

00:23:33.406 --> 00:23:35.408
- Yes.
- Does that work?

00:23:35.408 --> 00:23:37.610
- Yes, so far, but it might
change, and the kids are getting

00:23:37.610 --> 00:23:39.579
older and it's not working for
me. One of the biggest things

00:23:39.579 --> 00:23:42.248
that I wanna change going
forward is coming home around

00:23:42.248 --> 00:23:44.617
dinner, and eat with them,
and then go back out,

00:23:44.617 --> 00:23:47.553
and so that's something for
me. You know, like it evolves,

00:23:47.553 --> 00:23:50.156
it evolves, I'm taking seven
weeks vacation now instead

00:23:50.156 --> 00:23:53.125
of two, that's a lot,
that's a big change for me.

00:23:53.125 --> 00:23:56.128
It evolves, so between weekends
and seven weeks vacation,

00:23:56.128 --> 00:23:57.830
it's not like, you know
like, I don't know,

00:23:57.830 --> 00:24:00.333
that seems like, I feel lazy.

00:24:00.333 --> 00:24:02.034
- Yeah, man, you've got
to pick up your game.

00:24:02.034 --> 00:24:03.436
- I'll try.

00:24:03.436 --> 00:24:04.704
- How do you...

00:24:07.012 --> 00:24:09.148
You mentioned that you
don't have time for Netflix,

00:24:09.148 --> 00:24:10.049
you don't have time 
for this other stuff,

00:24:10.049 --> 00:24:11.237
but how do you balance
the time to figure out--

00:24:11.237 --> 00:24:12.118
- [Gary] But, real quick, 
I have time

00:24:12.118 --> 00:24:14.076
for the Jets for seven hours--

00:24:14.076 --> 00:24:14.911
- We know that.

00:24:14.911 --> 00:24:16.512
- [Gary] You know, 
16 times a year on a Sunday.

00:24:16.512 --> 00:24:18.147
Everybody needs escapism.

00:24:18.147 --> 00:24:19.081
You have to understand.

00:24:19.081 --> 00:24:20.549
This is a great opportunity
with a lot of people watching

00:24:20.549 --> 00:24:22.118
